// ===================== 16.6 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('16.6  The whole assessment'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **The second half is the diagnostic and the figures are worth collecting:** **the missing part is the appeal in about half of cases and the weighting in most of the rest.** **Nobody has ever been missing the verdict.** **Put that on the board** — **the two parts that protect the person assessed are the two that go missing, and the one that protects the assessor never does.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **2** — _criteria · weights · working · verdict · appeal._ ⚠ **Do not accept it as a convention.** **Each part is unreadable without the one above it:** **a verdict with no working is an assertion, working with no weights cannot be added up, weights with no criteria weigh nothing** — **and an appeal route above the verdict invites somebody to appeal against something they have not read.** **5 is the near miss and the room will choose it:** **the verdict third, before the working, which is 16.3 lost at the level of the whole page.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **the first two parts must have been published before anything was assessed**, and **the whole thing is one page**, because **the parts a reader cannot find are the parts that are not used.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **Read the page aloud as the person it is about, not as its author.** **She has been that person:** **in your own voice you look for yourself in it and you find a verdict; reading it as them, you find out whether there is anything they could have done.**   **2** ⚠ **The working is the long part and it is shorter than people expect** — _nine of eleven on the agreed date, from our goods-in book_ **is twelve words and is complete working.** **What makes assessments long is the prose round the working** — _we were impressed by_, _it was felt that_ — **and none of it is evidence.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The one-page rule is not a style preference and should not be taught as one.** **An appeal route on the fourth page is a route nobody takes**, so the length limit is what makes part five real. ⚠ **And the strike-out in the homework is the exercise that proves it:** **cross out every verb of feeling or impression** — _impressed_, _felt_, _on balance_, _considered carefully_ — **and read what is left.** **If the page still works, those words were doing nothing; if it collapses, there was no working in it.** **Expect about a third of a first draft to go and the working to survive untouched**, which is the finding to name out loud. ⚠ **Keep the whole-page test in Ms Huda\'s words rather than paraphrasing:** **is there anything on it the person could have done differently?** **If there is nothing, the page has told them they are the wrong kind of person** — **which is not what any of the five parts was for, and is the fault 16.1 began with.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(published)_ _Assessed against the three criteria given on 2 September, with the weighting given in the same note._',
  '**2** _(not published, said so)_ ⚠ _The weighting was not published and was agreed by the panel before the papers were read._ **Which costs something and is true.**',
  '**3** _(working in twelve words)_ _Nine of eleven on the agreed date, from our goods-in book._',
  '**4** _(nothing could have been done)_ ⚠ _Nothing you could have produced would have changed this one: the post was withdrawn on 2 March, before your papers were read._',
  '**5** _(the verdict first, so that you hear it)_ _Fail. Seven of eleven instructions in the imperative._ → ⚠ **and the evidence has become support for a decision already taken.**',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Item **2** is the item to collect, because **most students have a real one** and writing it is the hardest sentence in the cycle. Accept nothing hedged: _the weighting was not formally circulated_ **is the same fault in a smaller coat.** Item **3** is marked by counting: **twelve to fifteen words, a number, a source, and no verb of feeling.** Item **5** is said aloud, in both orders — **the words are identical and the room hears a case become a finding.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**A** _Assessed against the four criteria published on 2 February._',
  '**B** **Part 1.** **Right place** — **it is the first line.** **Before it: nothing.**',
  '**A** _(the ⚠ row)_ _We were impressed by the general standard of the submission._',
  '**B** ⚠ **It is pretending to be part 3 and it is not working at all.** **_Impressed_ has no object a third person could check.** **What would make it part 3:** _eight of the nine sections complete, from the submission itself._ **Before it: the criteria and the weights.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The part number for each line and what must come before it. ⚠ **The four ⚠ rows are prose pretending to be working and they all have the same fault:** **a verb of feeling or impression with no object a third person could check.** _We were impressed. It was felt. On balance. Careful consideration._ **None says what was looked at.** **Make the listeners name the fault in those words rather than saying _vague_** — **the diagnosis has to be portable to their own pages, and _vague_ is not.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**Five lines, and the count:** criteria **14 words** · weights **19** · working **41** · verdict **11** · appeal **28**. **113 in all.**_ ⚠ _**The strike-out:** _we were satisfied that_ **and** _on the whole_ **have gone, which is nine words, and the page reads better without them.** **The working did not lose a word.**_',
  '**b** ⚠ _**She read it as the supplier.** **Her answer to the one question: yes — send the machine log by 6 April.** **Which is on the page, and it is the only sentence on it I nearly left out.**_',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Adnan gave the five parts in order — criteria, weights, working, verdict, appeal — and said the first two were published before anything was looked at, which is the sentence the whole page rests on and goes in the first line: assessed against the four criteria published in the invitation of 2 February, with the weighting published in the same document. Twenty words, and everything after it is checkable. Rim asked what happens if they were not published, and then you have an assessment and not a defensible one, and the honest thing is to say so — the weighting was not published and was agreed by the panel before the papers were read. That sentence costs you something and it is true, which is more than the alternative. Omar asked how long the whole thing is, and one page: the parts a reader cannot find are the parts that are not used, and an appeal route on the fourth page is a route nobody takes. Dima said the working is the long part, and it is the long part and it is shorter than people expect — nine of eleven on the agreed date, from our goods-in book, is twelve words and is complete working. What makes assessments long is the prose round the working, and none of it is evidence. Hiba then gave the instruction about reading it: read the page aloud as the person it is about, because in your own voice you look for yourself in it and find a verdict, and in theirs you find out whether there is anything they could have done. Which is the test for the whole page in one question: is there anything on it the person could have done differently? If there is nothing, the page has told them they are the wrong kind of person, which is not what any of the five parts was for. Bassel asked what happens when the answer is genuinely no, and then you say that, which is 16.4 — nothing you could have produced would have changed this one, and here is why, which is a hard sentence and is kinder than silence, because the silence is read as something, but we are not saying what. And Mazen asked whether the verdict really goes fourth, and fourth, after the working, every time.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the answer the partner gave to the one question about the page. ⚠ **Take the _no_ answers first and do not let them pass as modesty.** **A page on which the person assessed could have done nothing is either a page that owes them 16.4\'s hard sentence — _nothing you could have produced would have changed this one, and here is why_ — or a page that has judged the person rather than the work.** **The two are easy to tell apart:** **the first names something outside their control and dates it; the second names nothing at all.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _We were impressed by the submission._ → **✓** eight of nine sections complete.   **✗** The verdict before the working. → **✓** fourth, after it, every time.   **✗** The weighting summarised. → **✓** quoted, with its date.   **✗** The appeal on the second page. → **✓** on the page, with a date.   **✗** _It was felt that._ → **✓** who looked at what.   **✗** Nothing the person could have done, and no sentence saying so. → **✓** say it, and why.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **On 14 March the plan said _review of the past perfect_ and the lesson spent thirty-one minutes on vocabulary.** **What points the other way is named and is genuinely good:** **those thirty-one minutes were the better part of that lesson.** **It does not change the verdict because the criterion is whether the plan predicts the lesson** — **and three of the four plans did, so the criterion passes.** **The concession is real and the criterion is still the criterion.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **Two routes.** **The condition:** **send the transcript of 21 March by 17 April** → **she counts the instructions in it and reassesses criterion three against three lessons rather than two** — **and that goes to nobody else; she records the reassessment on the file either way.** **The appeal:** **if Miss Zein thinks the plans were misread, say which lesson and send the plan** → **that goes to Mrs Sweidan, who did not observe her.** **The first admits a limit in her own evidence; the second puts the reading of it in somebody else\'s hands.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **Task range.** **It has passed everybody she has assessed under this form, which is four people, and she is rewriting it before September.** **And what she tells Miss Zein is the part to notice:** **you were told three types and you gave three types, so it has not cost you anything** — **but you should know which line on your page was not doing any work.** **That is 16.5 written inside the assessment, to the person assessed, which is what makes the other two criteria believable.**'));
